{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And Elohim made the rakia</b> — the language of making (עשייה) everywhere denotes the fashioning of a thing into its proper arrangement." "<b>And it was so</b> — in the first instance Scripture wrote “And there was light” after “And Elohim said, Let there be light”; after the utterance it explained that it went forth into actuality and was as had been decreed. But here, after the utterance “Let there be a rakia,” it is written, “And Elohim made the rakia and divided,” so why does it add “And it was so”? Rather, it is to say that it remained so forever, all the days of the world. And Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] explained that it is joined with what comes after it: when it was so, He called the rakia “shamayim.” But this is not correct. And in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 4:6) they said: “And Elohim made the rakia” — this is one of the verses through which Ben Zoma shook the world: “And Elohim made,” etc. — but is it not written, “By the word of YHWH the heavens were made” (Psalms 33:6)? And this shaking is not from the expression “and He made” alone, for on the fourth day and on the sixth day “and He made” is written. Rather, it was from what I have said: because on the rest of the days, after the utterance, “and it was so” is written, to say that it came to be so immediately, straightway after the utterance, whereas here after “And Elohim said” it is written “and He made” — this is his difficulty. And perhaps he had a hidden interpretation whose secret he did not wish to reveal, and this is the substance of the shaking:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-21T22:12:17.644572866Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}